<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Protest and freedom · 1952 · Sub-Saharan Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Siyahamba</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Zulu church song from South Africa whose name means &quot;We are marching&quot;. Children's choirs sing it all over the world.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>not known (Zulu words); Andries van Tonder credited with the earliest known Afrikaans version</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>not known (possibly Andries van Tonder, possibly a Zulu folk song)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Zulu Protestant congregations of South Africa; later churches and the anti-apartheid campaign worldwide</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Zulu, Afrikaans</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>possibly Natal, South Africa (transcribed at Dundee, Natal)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>possibly sung orally before 1952; first transcribed 1952</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words and melody of unknown authorship; first transcribed by Andries van Tonder, 1952 (death not known))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siyahamba">Siyahamba</a>&quot; is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> chorus that arose in a rural Protestant congregation, possibly in the former province of Natal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. It may have been sung orally before 1952, when Andries van Tonder of Dundee, Natal, first wrote it down. Van Tonder is credited with the earliest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> version of the words; who made the melody and the Zulu words is not known, and van Tonder may have composed it or it may be a Zulu folk song. Its words may draw on Methodist or Pentecostal hymns, while its melody is closer to African and African-American patterns than to conventional European church music. The title means &quot;We are marching&quot; or &quot;We are walking&quot; in Zulu.</p><p>In 1978 the Swedish choir Fjedur toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> at the invitation of the Evangelical Lutheran Church of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and its musical director, Anders Nyberg, heard and recorded the song at a girls' school in Appelsbosch, Natal. In 1984 Nyberg arranged it for four voices and published it in the songbook and recording Freedom is Coming: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> of Protest and Praise from South Africa. It then entered North American hymnals as &quot;We Are Marching in the Light of God&quot;: GIA Publications' Gather Comprehensive (1994), widely used in American Catholic parishes, the United Church of Christ's New Century Hymnal (1995), Community of Christ Sings (2002) and the Unitarian Universalist supplement Singing the Journey (2005).</p><p>The song was not written as a protest song and seems not to have been prominent among anti-apartheid campaigners inside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. After Nyberg brought it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the 1980s, however, it was often used in the international effort to end apartheid, above all in the campaign of the Western churches, because of its devotional message. Today it is seen in South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and abroad as a liberation song. It is sung in churches, at concerts and by children's groups, as a processional and at offertory, and at rallies, demonstrations and processions, where its words are sometimes changed to fit the cause, as they are in some secular performances.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of faith whose theme is a people moving forward together; its mood is joyful and steady, and its one repeated phrase gives it a cyclical, communal feel suited to processions.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so they are not printed here. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above gives their theme in our own words, and the performance below lets you hear them.</p><div class="sg-genius" data-genius="9882064"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn">Show the lyrics from Genius</button><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press the button. <a href="https://genius.com/Traditional-transcriptions-siyahamba-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
